## 14. Human cognition and learning-science policy

| Principle | Beacon policy | Rejected oversimplification |
|---|---|---|
| Working memory / cognitive load | Manage complexity relative to prior knowledge; progressively disclose. | "Never show more than four things." |
| Dual coding / multimedia | Combine verbal and visual when each contributes meaning. | "Always add an image." |
| Spatial contiguity / signaling | Keep labels near relevant visual when it reduces searching. | "More annotation is always better." |
| Segmenting | Let complex sequences unfold in learner-paced steps when needed. | "Everything must be a stepper." |
| Active/generative processing | Prefer prediction, explanation, construction, comparison. | "More clicking means more learning." |
| Retrieval practice | Use retrieval as strong evidence of learning. | "Retrieval always beats concept maps." |
| Feedback | Perceptually immediate for direct manipulation; task-dependent timing otherwise. | "All feedback must arrive within milliseconds." |
| Analogy | Use when structural mapping reduces conceptual distance; disclose breakpoints. | "Abstract concepts cannot be understood without analogy." |
| Concrete-to-abstract | Use concreteness fading when it fits; not a universal order. | "Every concept must follow the same sequence." |

### 14.1 Human memory vs Beacon memory

Beacon does not claim to store a human brain. It stores externalized intellectual
lineage so the human does not have to reconstruct every decision from memory.

---

## 15. Visual Understanding Router

{% mermaid_diagram src="/diagrams/mermaid/phase-1-6-visual-understanding-router.mmd" title="Visual Understanding Router" /%}
*Figure 7. Representation is chosen by both knowledge structure and learning intent.*

| Knowledge / learning need | Preferred representation |
|---|---|
| Hierarchy / concept landscape | Markmap or equivalent mind-map projection. |
| Process / architecture / sequence / state / ERD | Mermaid canonical diagram. |
| Exploratory design reasoning | Excalidraw, clearly marked exploratory/non-authoritative. |
| Alternatives / evidence / readiness | Beacon-native matrix or checklist. |
| Evolution of understanding | Timeline with version diffs. |
| Quantitative behavior | Chart with explicit source/provenance. |
| Causal variable relationship | Bounded interactive simulation if manipulation reveals mechanism. |
| Text is clearest | Use text. No mandatory visual. |

---

## 16. Diagram architecture and representation canon

| Diagram type | Canonical use | Authority rule |
|---|---|---|
| Markmap | Concept landscapes, prerequisite trees, research question structures. | Learning/navigation projection; not implementation authority. |
| Mermaid flow/sequence/state/ERD/architecture | Canonical technical relationships and execution flows. | May become canonical when linked to accepted ADR/docs. |
| Excalidraw | Exploration, analogy, whiteboarding, uncertain architecture. | Always exploratory until promoted through an accepted decision. |
| Native Beacon matrix/timeline | Evidence, alternatives, readiness, evolution, status. | Derived projection from canonical objects. |
| Interactive model | Causal exploration where state manipulation materially improves understanding. | Explanatory, never execution authority by itself. |

### 16.1 Promotion from exploratory to formal architecture

```
Excalidraw exploration
  -> evidence / alternatives
  -> Decision Candidate
  -> human acceptance
  -> accepted ADR
  -> canonical Mermaid architecture / docs
```

---

## 17. Active understanding, retrieval and feedback

- Predict what happens next.
- Change one variable and explain the consequence.
- Explain the mechanism in plain language.
- Retrieve a core relationship without rereading.
- Compare two models and identify where each fails.
- Apply the concept to a new Beacon scenario.
- Diagnose an intentionally incorrect architecture or claim.

| Evidence | State |
|---|---|
| Can identify core components | yes / no / unknown |
| Can explain simply | yes / no / unknown |
| Can predict behavior | yes / no / unknown |
| Can distinguish credible alternatives | yes / no / unknown |
| Can identify boundary cases | yes / no / unknown |
| Can apply to a new case | yes / no / unknown |
| Can explain why a rejected option fails | yes / no / unknown |

> Do not collapse these into an invented 94% understanding score. Unknown remains
> unknown.

---

## 18. Decision readiness and Decision Candidate

```
DecisionReadiness {
  problemDefined,
  currentStateVerified,
  primaryEvidencePresent,
  credibleAlternativesCompared,
  contradictionsHandled,
  securityConsidered,
  costComplexityConsidered,
  architectureImpactKnown,
  experimentDispositionKnown,
  revisitConditionsDefined,
  readyForAdr
}
```

| Disposition | Meaning |
|---|---|
| Accept | Create `DecisionAccepted` and begin ADR request; still not executable until ADR accepted. |
| Continue Research | Return to the Research Thread with explicit unresolved questions. |
| Reject | Record why the candidate was rejected; preserve evidence and reconsideration triggers. |

---

## 19. ADR authority boundary

{% mermaid_diagram src="/diagrams/mermaid/phase-1-6-adr-authority-boundary.mmd" title="ADR authority boundary" /%}
*Figure 8. Only a human-accepted ADR crosses the research-to-execution authority boundary.*

> **AUTHORITY INVARIANT.** Research != authorization. Evidence != authorization.
> Concept != authorization. Diagram != authorization. Recommendation != authorization.
> Decision Candidate != authorization. Only an accepted ADR authorizes execution.

---

## 20. Compact Decision Package and Phase 1.5 handoff

```
DecisionPackage {
  adrId,
  objective,
  acceptanceCriteria[],
  invariants[],
  constraints[],
  securityRequirements[],
  risk,
  architectureRefs[],
  documentationRefs[],
  evidenceRefs[],
  unresolvedRisks[],
  revisitConditions[]
}
```

Phase 1.5 then performs its existing Chief-of-Staff → WorkUnit → context compiler →
provider router → eight agents → QA/review/council → CI/publication/release
pipeline. Phase 1.6 must not bypass this path.

---

## 21. End-to-end provenance and auditability

{% mermaid_diagram src="/diagrams/mermaid/phase-1-6-provenance-flow.mmd" title="End-to-end provenance flow" /%}
*Figure 9. Forward provenance from source to production; every step is traceable backward as well.*

### 21.1 Provenance invariants

- Every Claim references Evidence; every Evidence references a Source or project
  artifact.
- Every UnderstandingVersion records its evidence set and project-context hash.
- Every Decision Candidate references its source UnderstandingVersion.
- Every accepted ADR links back to the Decision Candidate and forward to
  DecisionPackage/WorkUnits.
- Every deployment/result references the immutable artifact and originating decision
  lineage.
- Retrospectives can create new claims/evidence and therefore new
  UnderstandingVersions.

---

## 22. Terminal UX

The terminal is the primary control plane.

```
$ beacon
Project     Beacon-Co
Phase       1.6
Knowledge   synchronized
Providers   Phase 1.5 broker
Provider    Claude (approaching limit — 12% remaining)     ← new in V5
Continuity  fresh (observed 3m ago)                         ← new in V5

> Would a knowledge graph materially improve our retrieval?

Beacon:
- captured thought
- attached current project context
- found existing related thread: knowledge-relations
- 3 unresolved questions
- research can continue without opening /lab
```

**On an automatic provider switch (new in V5):**
```
Beacon: Claude capacity reached mid-task.
  → Continuation package generated (observedAt: 2026-08-10T14:32:01Z)
  → Switching to Codex
  → Codex: context loaded, resuming from last WorkUnit step
  → No user action required.
```

**When both providers are unavailable (new in V5):**
```
Beacon: Both Claude and Codex are at capacity.
  → Work paused, ContinuationPackage saved and current.
  → No provider action possible right now.
  → Resume with `beacon continue` once either provider is available.
```

### 22.1 Core commands

| Command | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `beacon status` | Phase, provider, readiness, current WorkUnit/Research Thread. |
| `beacon knowledge <topic>` | Retrieve current understanding and linked evidence. |
| `beacon research <question>` | Create/continue a Research Thread. |
| `beacon explain <concept>` | Compile an Understanding Package. |
| `beacon decision` | Inspect decision readiness and current candidate. |
| `beacon continue` | Resume from provider-neutral continuation state — re-verifies freshness before trusting gate status (V5). |
| `beacon lineage <id>` | Trace thought/evidence/ADR/implementation/outcome provenance. |

---

## 23. Beacon Lab UX

`/lab` is a rich projection for learning and review — not the canonical database, not
a manual wiki maintenance surface.

### 23.1 Concept / thread page
- Original question and current status
- 30-second model
- Question Tree and related concepts
- Current UnderstandingVersion with version history
- Claim Ledger: supported / limited / contested / rejected
- Evidence matrix with source type, quality and freshness
- Best-fit diagrams and interactive explanation when justified
- Boundary conditions and unresolved questions
- Observable understanding checks
- Alternatives and Decision Readiness
- Linked ADRs, PRs, WorkUnits, deployments and outcomes

### 23.2 Progressive disclosure

The first viewport should orient, not overwhelm.

---

## 24. Private, formal and public projections

| Projection | Visibility | Content policy |
|---|---|---|
| Beacon Lab / Brain | Private by default | Raw questions, incomplete research, contradictions, experiments, internal reasoning. |
| Decisions / Docs | Internal/canonical | Accepted ADRs and current implementation truth. |
| Building Beacon | Public curated | Sanitized narrative only; no raw prompts, secrets, customer data, security-sensitive details, unverified claims. |

> **PUBLICATION IS A PROJECTION.** Never make public publication the storage
> mechanism for private intellectual history.

---

## 25. Canonical data model and ERD

{% mermaid_diagram src="/diagrams/mermaid/phase-1-6-canonical-data-model.mmd" title="Canonical data model (ERD)" /%}
*Figure 10. Simplified Phase 1.6 knowledge-to-authority data relationships, extended with ContinuationPackage tracking (V5).*

Initial persistence should use explicit typed relations and generated indexes that
extend the existing Markdoc/project-memory system. A graph database is not required
until measured query/retrieval requirements justify it.

### 25.1 Minimum canonical entities

- PromptObserved / ThoughtClassification
- ResearchThread / Question
- ProjectContextSnapshot
- Source / Evidence
- Claim / ClaimRelation
- UnderstandingVersion / MentalModel / UnderstandingCheck
- Alternative / Experiment
- DecisionReadiness / DecisionCandidate
- ADRRef / DecisionPackage
- ExecutionRef / OutcomeRef / Retrospective
- **ContinuationPackage** *(promoted to canonical entity in V5 — was implicit in V4)*

